    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p><strong>Is an under desk elliptical as good as walking?</strong> Minute for minute, no. Walking at an ordinary 3 mph pace takes more energy than pedaling in a chair, because your legs have to carry your body weight. But "as good as walking" depends on what you are measuring, and on a full workday the seated machine often comes out ahead simply because you can use it for hours.</p>
<p>The short version: a 30-minute walk burns more than 30 minutes of seated pedaling, but two hours of easy pedaling while you work can match or beat that walk in calories. Walking still wins for bone loading, balance and posture. The best plan for most desk workers is to do both. Below is the math, so you can see exactly how the two compare.</p>

<h2>Under Desk Elliptical vs Walking: The MET Comparison</h2>
<p>A MET (metabolic equivalent of task) is a measure of how hard an activity is compared with resting. One MET is roughly the energy you use sitting quietly. The Compendium of Physical Activities, the reference most exercise researchers use, gives these approximate values:</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Activity</th><th>Approximate METs</th><th>Compared with sitting</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Sitting at a desk</td><td>1.3</td><td>Baseline</td></tr>
<tr><td>Light seated pedaling (typing pace)</td><td>2.0</td><td>About 1.5× sitting</td></tr>
<tr><td>Moderate seated pedaling</td><td>2.8</td><td>About 2× sitting</td></tr>
<tr><td>Slow walking (around 2 mph)</td><td>2.8</td><td>About 2× sitting</td></tr>
<tr><td>Walking at 3 mph</td><td>3.5</td><td>About 2.7× sitting</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Two things stand out. First, an ordinary walk is harder than seated pedaling, by roughly 25 percent (against moderate pedaling) to 75 percent (against light pedaling). Second, moderate pedaling is in the same range as a slow stroll. If your idea of "walking" is a gentle wander, a seated elliptical at moderate resistance is a fair match.</p>

<h2>Calories: How Does It Compare to Walking?</h2>
<p>To turn METs into calories, use the standard formula: calories per minute = MET × 3.5 × body weight in kg ÷ 200. Here is what that gives for a 155 lb (70 kg) person over one hour:</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Activity (60 minutes)</th><th>Calories burned</th><th>Extra over sitting</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Sitting</td><td>about 95</td><td>0</td></tr>
<tr><td>Light seated pedaling (2.0 METs)</td><td>about 145</td><td>about 50</td></tr>
<tr><td>Moderate seated pedaling (2.8 METs)</td><td>about 205</td><td>about 110</td></tr>
<tr><td>Walking at 3 mph (3.5 METs)</td><td>about 255</td><td>about 160</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>The "extra over sitting" column is the useful one, because you would be sitting anyway. A 30-minute walk adds about 80 calories above sitting. You get the same extra from about 45 minutes of moderate pedaling or about 95 minutes of light pedaling. Heavier people burn more in every row; lighter people burn less.</p>
<h3>Why time on the machine changes the answer</h3>
<p>Most people take one or two short walks in a workday at best. An under desk elliptical has no such limit, because you use it while working. If you pedal lightly for two hours, which many owners report doing across meetings and email, you add roughly 100 extra calories. Push that to moderate effort and you are well past what a lunchtime walk would give you.</p>
<p>That is why the honest answer to the question "is an under desk elliptical as good as walking" is: not per minute, but often per day. For more on realistic burn rates, see <a href="/does-under-desk-elliptical-burn-calories/">does an under desk elliptical burn calories</a>.</p>

<h2>Is It Equal to Walking in Steps?</h2>
<p>Steps are where the comparison gets messy. Most wrist trackers count steps from arm swing, and your arms stay still while you pedal seated. So your watch may record almost nothing for an hour on an elliptical, even though you were working.</p>
<p>Some under desk ellipticals count strides on their own display, but a stride on a seated machine is not the same effort as a walking step. The most honest way to compare is by time and intensity. Use the converter below to see a step equivalent based on how long and how hard you pedal:</p>
[[tool:steps]]
<p>If your tracker lets you add workouts manually, log your sessions as "elliptical" or "cycling" so the activity counts toward your daily totals. Our guide to <a href="/does-under-desk-elliptical-count-as-steps/">whether an under desk elliptical counts as steps</a> covers the main trackers.</p>

<h2>What Walking Does That Seated Pedaling Doesn't</h2>
<p>Calories are only part of the picture. Walking has some benefits a seated machine cannot copy:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Weight-bearing load.</strong> Carrying your body weight helps maintain bone density, especially as you age.</li>
<li><strong>Balance and coordination.</strong> Every step trains small stabilizing muscles in your feet, ankles and hips.</li>
<li><strong>Posture and hip extension.</strong> Walking opens up the hips that stay bent all day when you sit.</li>
<li><strong>Outdoor time.</strong> A walk outside adds daylight and a mental break that a desk machine does not.</li>
</ul>
<p>So when you ask whether an under desk elliptical is as good as walking for overall health, it is a strong supplement to walking, not a full replacement. If you can only do one, walking is the better all-round activity. If you are stuck at a desk for most of the day, the elliptical fills a gap that walking cannot.</p>

<h2>What Seated Pedaling Does Better</h2>
<p>The seated machine has advantages of its own, and for some people they matter more than the higher burn of walking.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>It fits into work time.</strong> You do not need to stop working, change shoes or go outside.</li>
<li><strong>It breaks up long sitting.</strong> Frequent light movement through the day is one of the ideas behind NEAT (non-exercise activity thermogenesis) research associated with the Mayo Clinic. Small movements across many hours add up.</li>
<li><strong>It is gentle on joints.</strong> Your weight stays on the chair, so it may suit people with sore knees, hips or feet, or anyone who finds walking painful.</li>
<li><strong>Weather never stops it.</strong> Rain, heat, ice and darkness do not matter.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you have a health condition, ask your doctor which kind of activity suits you best. For a broader look at what regular use may do for you, read our guide to <a href="/under-desk-elliptical-benefits/">under desk elliptical benefits</a>.</p>

<h3>Who benefits most from choosing the seated option</h3>
<p>The seated machine makes the most sense for people who cannot easily walk more: those with long, back-to-back meeting days, people with foot or knee pain that limits walking, older adults who want low-risk movement, and anyone in a climate where outdoor walking is hard for months at a time. If none of those apply and you can simply walk more, do that first. See our picks for the <a href="/best-under-desk-elliptical-for-seniors/">best under desk elliptical for seniors</a> if gentle movement is the goal.</p>

<h2>How to Make an Under Desk Elliptical More Like Walking</h2>
<p>If you want to close the gap between seated pedaling and a real walk, these changes help.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Raise resistance during calls and reading.</strong> Moving from light to moderate effort lifts you from about 2.0 to 2.8 METs, which is the same range as a slow walk.</li>
<li><strong>Pedal longer.</strong> Aim for several 10 to 20 minute blocks across the day. A <a href="/tools/desk-pedal-timer/">desk pedal timer</a> keeps you on track.</li>
<li><strong>Sit tall and push through your heels.</strong> Good posture and a full stride bring your hamstrings and glutes into the work.</li>
<li><strong>Add short walks too.</strong> Even two 10-minute walks a day give you the weight-bearing benefits the machine cannot.</li>
</ol>
<div class="callout"><p><strong>Realistic target:</strong> The CDC and WHO recommend at least 150 minutes of moderate activity a week. Seated pedaling at moderate effort plus a few brisk walks is a practical way for desk workers to get there.</p></div>

<h2>A Sample Day That Matches a Daily Walk</h2>
<p>Here is what a realistic day looks like if your goal is to match the calorie value of a 30 to 45 minute walk using a seated machine, based on the numbers above for a 155 lb person.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Time of day</th><th>What you do</th><th>Effort</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Morning email (20 min)</td><td>Pedal at light resistance</td><td>Light</td></tr>
<tr><td>Team call (30 min)</td><td>Turn resistance up a notch</td><td>Moderate</td></tr>
<tr><td>Focused work (2 hours)</td><td>Pedal in short bursts or pause</td><td>Light, on and off</td></tr>
<tr><td>Afternoon slump (20 min)</td><td>Steady moderate pedaling</td><td>Moderate</td></tr>
<tr><td>Reading or webinar (30 min)</td><td>Light to moderate</td><td>Light to moderate</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>That adds up to roughly two hours of movement and covers the extra calories of a typical daily walk, without taking any time out of your schedule. Add a short real walk at lunch and you get the best of both.</p>
<h3>Is a Cubii as good as walking?</h3>
<p>People often ask this about the Cubii specifically, since it is the best-known brand. The answer is the same as for any seated elliptical: lighter per minute, easier to keep up for long periods. The Cubii's quiet magnetic resistance makes those long, low-effort sessions easier to sustain during calls, which is a big part of why it can add up. Read more in our <a href="/cubii-under-desk-elliptical-is-it-really-worth-using-every-day/">Cubii everyday-use guide</a>.</p>

<h2>Prefer Real Steps? Consider a Walking Pad</h2>
<p>If walking is what you really want and you have a standing desk, a walking pad gives you true steps while you work. The [[amazon:walkingpad-c2|WalkingPad C2]] folds away when you are done. The trade-off is noise, cost and harder typing. Our <a href="/under-desk-elliptical-vs-walking-pad/">under desk elliptical vs walking pad</a> comparison covers it in detail.</p>
